th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/aleph-null.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/aleph-null.html","questions":[{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is a standard example of cardinality $\\aleph_0$?","options":["$2^{\\mathbb{N}}$","$\\mathbb{Z}$","$\\mathbb{R}$","$[0,1]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0d2a4a379832cfb4d9a9a42","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can $\\mathbb{Q}$ have cardinality $\\aleph_0$ even though it is dense in the real line?","options":["Density is topological, while $\\aleph_0$ concerns listing","Density implies a set is finite","Rational numbers are not real numbers","Every dense subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has cardinality $\\mathfrak{c}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0b7171f94ab898b6dc9739f6","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a bijection from $\\mathbb{N}$ to a set $A$ really provide?","options":["A proof that $A$ is finite","An ordering of $A$ by numerical size","A proof that $A$ contains all real numbers.","An enumeration of $A$ with no omissions or repeats"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b447c3384dc2d43b13e558f8","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the cardinality of $\\mathbb{N}\\times\\{0,1\\}$?","options":["$2$","$\\aleph_0$","$2^{\\aleph_0}$","$\\mathfrak{c}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2056613fd4d80b629d9328c4","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"An infinite subset $S$ of $\\mathbb{N}$ is written in increasing order as $s_1 \\lt s_2 \\lt s_3 \\lt \\cdots$. What does this show?","options":["$S$ has cardinality $\\aleph_0$","$S$ has cardinality $\\mathfrak{c}$","$S$ is finite because it is bounded by $\\mathbb{N}$","$S$ must equal $\\mathbb{N}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2882c561f56c176328a282d1","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_011_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A student argues: '$\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ are both infinite, so both have cardinality $\\aleph_0$.' What is wrong with this argument?","options":["Infinite sets can differ in size: $\\mathbb{R}$ cannot be listed","Nothing, because every infinite set has cardinality $\\aleph_0$","Nothing, because $\\aleph_0$ is just the calculus symbol $\\infty$","$\\mathbb{R}$ has no cardinality, since its elements cannot be listed"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5af61e7e4963b7b2ba5154d0","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_016_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be countably infinite and let $B$ be a set of five elements, disjoint from $A$. What is $\\operatorname{card}(A\\cup B)$?","options":["$\\aleph_0$","Strictly larger than $\\aleph_0$","It depends on which five elements are in $B$","$\\mathfrak{c}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e0e10c2b817143d070c7e080","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_018_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"With $\\mathbb{N}=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$, which map $f\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{Z}$ is a bijection?","options":["$f(n)=n/2$ for even $n$, $f(n)=-(n+1)/2$ for odd $n$","$f(n)=n$ for even $n$, $f(n)=-n$ for odd $n$","$f(n)=n/2$ for even $n$, $f(n)=-n$ for odd $n$","$f(n)=n/2$ for even $n$, $f(n)=(n+1)/2$ for odd $n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7bcb0abb7eda5438dd70cba6","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch22_aleph_null_019_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\operatorname{card}(\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N})$?","options":["$\\aleph_0$","Strictly larger than $\\aleph_0$, as it holds infinitely many copies of $\\mathbb{N}$","$\\mathfrak{c}$, as it is a two-dimensional grid like $\\mathbb{R}^2$","Uncountable, as listing it row by row never finishes the first row"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9748122d4b293bdb7595ea93","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_aleph_null_c5520157_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A set $A$ can be written as a sequence $a_0,a_1,a_2,\\ldots$ in which every element of $A$ appears at least once, but repetitions are allowed. Which conclusion about $A$ is justified?","options":["$A$ has cardinality $\\aleph_0$","Nothing, since only a bijection with $\\mathbb{N}$ says anything about size","$A$ is uncountable, since the list is not a bijection","$A$ is finite or has cardinality $\\aleph_0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4f365fcd9b0890f609ec7d63","time_limit":40}]}}